nd column 6 points simultaneously while being sunny. So we cannot have a line that covers column 6 point (6,1) and also column 5 point. So the line covering (6,1) must be a line that covers column 3 point and maybe column 4 point? Let's check possibilities.\n\nWe need to cover (6,1) with a sunny line. That line can intersect column 3 at some point (3,y3). The slope m = (1 - y3) / (6-3) = (1 - y3)/3. This slope must not be 0, -1, infinite. So y3 cannot be 1 (slope 0) and cannot be 4 (since (1-4)/3 = -1, slope -1). So y3 can be 2 or 3. So possible lines covering (6,1) are:\n\n- L_a: through (3,2) and (6,1): slope = (1-2)/3 = -1/3, allowed. Equation: y - 2 = -1/3 (x - 3) => y = -1/3 x + 3 + 2 = -1/3 x + 5. At x=4, y = -4/3 + 5 = 11/3 ≈ 3.667, not integer. At x=5, y = -5/3 + 5 = 10/3 ≈ 3.333, not integer. So this line does not cover any required point in column 4 or 5.\n\n- L_b: through (3,3) and (6,1): slope = (1-3)/3 = -2/3, allowed. Equation: y - 3 = -2/3 (x - 3) => y = -2/3 x + 2 + 3 = -2/3 x + 5. At x=4, y = -8/3 + 5 = 7/3 ≈ 2.333, not integer. At x=5, y = -10/3 + 5 = 5/3 ≈ 1.667, not integer. So no required points.\n\nThus the line covering (6,1) cannot also cover any required point in columns 4 or 5. So that line will only cover (3,y3) and (6,1). That's okay; we can assign it to cover (3,2) or (3,3) and (6,1). So we need to allocate one line for (6,1) and a column 3 point.\n\nNow we need to cover column 5 points (5,1) and (5,2). These must be covered by lines that also cover column 3 points (since we have only 4 lines total, and each line already has a column 3 point). So we need to assign two of the remaining three lines (excluding the one covering (6,1)) to also cover column 5 points. Let's see if we can find lines that pass through (3,y3) and (5,y5) with allowed slope.\n\nSlope m = (y5 - y3) / (5-3) = (y5 - y3)/2. Must not be 0, -1. So y5 - y3 cannot be 0 (i.e., y5 ≠ y3) and cannot be -2 (i.e., y5 ≠ y3 - 2). Also slope cannot be infinite, not relevant.\n\nNow 